#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231830} :activity/id #uuid "1173ef06-74e3-5b10-8cad-8816bd626835" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231985 :tutorial/content "A standard vibrating system under an external force ![](images/f6/f6ec0c498eee67c8bceaf8cf937f9b77f6395bd4ad28df6da30ca42622366f99.svg) looks like\n![](images/bd/bd2f22daabf1b333259361452745a963a4fd08c93cf70178eb0fb7a64218fdd1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/2c/2cb3a34686b80c6ea9a5a2e433d1f804b765e1174532b15435f471746ffbd3af.svg) is the system's natural **internal frequency**.\n\nThe complementary solution ![](images/02/024420a1f4a1300a242618c4742c03a91e27ee710937bb23acd902722711833c.svg) describes the system's internal oscillation when there is no external force. It can be written as\n![](images/95/95b99f0f2a3303b1ae2b3cafa4d0df18925f01ed684f71311bda48ace70c1b4b.svg)\nso the motion naturally oscillates at frequency ![](images/70/70a39e6c2338362b95e1ca575f5881d5c55759902897a19735596bc717c7afcc.svg)\n\nIf the external force ![](images/f6/f6ec0c498eee67c8bceaf8cf937f9b77f6395bd4ad28df6da30ca42622366f99.svg) is periodic, then it has its own forcing frequency ![](images/9d/9d61bc8820c203579d5753afc12cf659e4aaef1e7e7d0ab448c34719972a3e73.svg) **Resonance** occurs when the forcing frequency matches the natural frequency:\n![](images/24/24b644830cfc3be4d93f3a97f082c3257d5fb20c016daa74db4ebf21eef732e9.svg)\n\nIntuitively, resonance happens because the force repeatedly \"pushes\" the system at exactly the right rhythm each cycle. In the undamped model, these pushes continually add energy to the system at just the right phase, causing the oscillation amplitude to grow without bound.\n\nFor example, suppose the position ![](images/a1/a11c6d83010d49a22f82075f22ecafa791d24bef37e65fa46d4bce6a795ac213.svg) measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n![](images/70/7076ff3f6735079d869815f4e815b7904a1a083fbe5a2e5d2281adfa270c375e.svg)\nwhere ![](images/dd/dde902d242b218395adbef9bfc212e62665b9dd9f239fc735fab8eb7325d2175.svg) is the time, in seconds. Let's find the value of the parameter ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) that causes the oscillator to resonate.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution has frequency ![](images/12/123dfb2143795c6c18189c8c267c39923598d8998875b966ce5a58a50796b170.svg) where ![](images/63/63b6e1d88c2e6c5a4db5966b5c6711403b91091065c54ae909620e0aa2a00111.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/04/0435ca12e088811ffad2b9fcc7d54a58b69f590bcb85657807e16eced9dd6470.svg)\n\nThe external force is\n![](images/19/191e5f6b5169e36e08215529683d79bad0046a39058c73741d4102c095bdf3bd.svg)\nwhich has forcing frequency ![](images/bf/bf89fc164205f4db41f1307668c4f114944b3478bd4c6c5f04dcda6452b67c7f.svg)\n\nResonance occurs when ![](images/bc/bc1f9bcb484213078132916bc6c9a42689f95ea470453d810294900e25c8597e.svg) so\n![](images/4f/4fec299df0cb32cc9077f3a3438885e1aa1d2f9db239d82c4f5b8a437ec04f93.svg)\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "b61c68de-4314-5d91-bcb2-75fd1ed98232" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "358b4213-7b6b-5471-a600-b592535b19ee" :step/math-academy-id 612647 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231831}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231404 :question/id #uuid "bb405211-5b18-5cc1-a264-bdf6e2fbcc60" :question/math-academy-id "e-6735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518148}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"} :step/id #uuid "4d89af0c-6f61-561d-ba3a-9fe8f3ec929d" :step/math-academy-id 612648 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231832}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231294 :question/id #uuid "7ad46566-b122-5de3-9b87-9beec9ac6761" :question/math-academy-id "e-21877"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495094}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"} :step/id #uuid "5658d94f-9b6c-586a-9fb4-a29a2128db9e" :step/math-academy-id 612651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231987 :tutorial/content "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation $y''+\\omega^2y=0$ and $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c,$ so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D,$ we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+t(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t))-t{\\omega }^{2}(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}{y}_{p}=2\\omega D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)-2\\omega C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we need $y_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),$ equating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a,\n\\qquad\n-2\\omega C=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac{a}{2\\omega},\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSo, when $f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)$ and resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b19a33b7-fd81-538c-8139-d44653c47533" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14777 :tutorial/title "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations With Resonance"} :step/id #uuid "35c65152-fe66-57ca-8c82-a01849b005d7" :step/math-academy-id 612654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231295 :question/id #uuid "f41473ff-05a6-5f3e-ae5b-0584810aa0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21878"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442740}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "eb6f14ff-8487-50a4-a45b-1ff02e60483d" :step/math-academy-id 612655 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231988 :tutorial/content "Recall that resonance is the phenomenon where an external periodic force drives a vibrating system at exactly the system's natural frequency. In the undamped model, this causes the oscillations to grow larger and larger over time.\n\nIn the ideal undamped model, resonance produces a particular solution that grows in magnitude with time (due to the extra factor of $t$). This is why the model predicts that the amplitude increases *without bound*.\n\nFor example, consider the undamped forced oscillator\n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = \\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution is of the form $y_c = A\\cos(3t) + B\\sin(3t),$ the natural frequency of the system is $\\omega = 3,$ and the forcing frequency is also $3.$ Because these frequencies match, resonance occurs.\n\nSince the forcing term $\\sin(3t)$ duplicates a term in the homogeneous solution, we must multiply our guess by $t.$ We try a solution of the form $y_p = t(C\\cos(3t) + D\\sin(3t)).$\n\nCalculating the derivatives of $y_p,$ substituting them into the equation, and equating coefficients of $\\sin(3t)$ and $\\cos(3t)$ yields $C=-\\dfrac16$ and $D=0.$ So, the particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = -\\dfrac{t}{6}\\cos(3t).\n$$\n\nNotice that this solution grows larger as $t$ increases. This time-dependent growth illustrates how resonance leads to an increase in oscillation amplitude in the undamped model.\n\nIn real systems, perfectly *unbounded growth* does not occur because physical effects, such as damping and energy loss, eventually limit the amplitude. Even so, forcing near the natural frequency can still produce very large oscillations, which is why resonance is important in engineering design." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f439c3c-eac7-5d41-a8b1-fcbe2eb8d44b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14778 :tutorial/title "Practical Resonance"} :step/id #uuid "d14184e9-93ef-5eca-99aa-b25660380309" :step/math-academy-id 612658}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231520}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441792} :activity/id #uuid "14d1651e-04e2-5488-8374-7db989fc704e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5551b954-fda2-5f2d-abc7-527dd2646792" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2595439f-c010-51c2-987d-b6e986cd13da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441794}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} {:answer/id #uuid "282a52cc-8658-5a56-b555-abd92b1826a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa88ca7-4486-52c4-a8ca-dfdd856acf42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7756f2f0-f8ff-5a38-a573-589b8cc562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343691" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c212f16-03c8-5837-8c98-8164c674583d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498bed50-28c7-50fa-a5df-0080495261c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abd7c36-14a4-56ef-ada9-6c206b80a9d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "968f331f-040e-5a16-a459-23a91c08dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343473" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77f21bd5-7692-5988-9811-98041bd1e682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14290d8c-e867-59b8-af1c-fccb11ec1cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474786} {:answer/id #uuid "ef81b3fc-df53-5633-a594-3d4f04f95619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474786} :answer-field/id #uuid "838696b9-c509-58f9-8a57-3688030a7f6e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475319} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc17cc0-79a7-5ee7-b21f-ee6d25df87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474788} {:answer/id #uuid "92aa028c-6b7c-580c-9ad9-b43fe721b886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474788} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3a8464-3e27-51fe-98f3-9d46f96ac2c0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475320} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa752ff5-8a8a-57e6-b3fb-ad9d131bd5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474790} {:answer/id #uuid "ee39a1b7-26b9-54e9-966d-579fbbb6a80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474791} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b5e26ac-beb1-59bd-879a-7f19ce8bf81d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343753" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution III matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c0f6cdf0-cd51-5bdc-9771-44211b216c7d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d69105-ab2d-55c3-9612-ee987e634694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8b9ffd-965a-5c5b-8676-57da40fbb9a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31720ab0-69d4-52f5-b3cb-fcd1e2ec0d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467258} {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b86baf-0e5a-524c-b103-8dac5824fae3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} {:answer/id #uuid "6239f5bd-e810-5e80-93e2-7e27a749a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5a3574-1760-5b93-87fd-b5f37a0870f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "de131a05-d058-5098-9651-430e6d375558" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441795}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441789}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442742} :activity/id #uuid "d9d2f46d-5baa-5bef-85ef-5cb27486297b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8098c12c-c69f-5410-91ea-5d34faacac72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474792} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf8c1e6-7440-5b66-ac6a-a95e969d0c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474793} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0703231-e8db-5753-a623-8cdd5ed3035a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebb008e-2a78-528f-b65f-04738d8d69bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474794} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5fb8cd-1e60-5d1a-9b3c-43f1bdfb0eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474794} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee39774-5b22-57d7-b22d-32c67ba80ab1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154527e1-d8e6-580a-bfa6-88b0d7e11c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474796} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8f9ab4-11d0-5d2b-b95a-f6633b354687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474797} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d02f47-2169-5889-be5d-7762353c0de6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5e9f5eb-87cf-56db-acd3-a7af3f0549dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-343755" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "102631d9-03a3-5a77-8b43-d3ebf3037f1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} {:answer/id #uuid "282a52cc-8658-5a56-b555-abd92b1826a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa88ca7-4486-52c4-a8ca-dfdd856acf42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7756f2f0-f8ff-5a38-a573-589b8cc562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343691" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5ee37f4e-428d-5c80-b898-6258e65e6514" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f62d1759-0498-58c4-ab55-caa874c6319e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467268} {:answer/id #uuid "d1649d8f-ee2f-5778-b299-fa2795a13fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467269} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d72ca48-f0c0-538a-84c8-10419eca178e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce9985f7-ada6-59aa-9213-ea46b2f0103a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467270} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5c8f3b-63a5-5160-a7b3-de3cdcbe35f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467271} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70a8fc2-cc6d-5e11-8840-935b2c760d12" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "891fbb34-02a0-5178-be8c-59a9c40ccee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467272} {:answer/id #uuid "b5910d95-689b-5498-970e-cb215a933405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467273} :answer-field/id #uuid "1174ed98-32d3-5eef-b1f3-eb94a6edeefc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceda7f80-0812-553b-8a96-78a8d37d4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "63616043-21be-5d80-99e6-bbe839235312"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c18230a-149d-58ba-93cf-40b239913f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435697} {:answer/id #uuid "065c1236-c8cd-5e0a-8536-ab48d1fe6aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442733} :answer-field/id #uuid "75523a18-6820-5b9b-bc7e-e9376c4fa924" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "189994a2-e868-5656-b67a-5717b59ea689" :question/math-academy-id "q-343698" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "83ab17b5-350b-529d-97cb-ee01d416ef71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ac45f2-8682-5e38-90a5-af6b3b0df052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442734} {:answer/id #uuid "720de89b-25be-516b-9df5-32bca90894e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442735} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3b3ddc-f2b0-5960-a120-6e3182615388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} {:answer/id #uuid "90774d44-8df7-5a01-b829-eff4553b5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442737} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5401b3-3249-566f-8e5f-01020ae0590b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3260424-876c-50ae-ac8b-7b96731c9907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee642054-1d27-50a4-8f05-345ca578dfc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343743" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 9D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c4c6cf6d-8b3b-5c57-b551-475a3ee53347" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442741}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442732}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481727} :activity/id #uuid "5cff95fc-6f6c-5d25-ae4b-a780b9efb5c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427258} :answer-field/id #uuid "96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077" :question/math-academy-id "q-343675" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is $4,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4166c13b-ac53-51f9-9738-1fcaff73fb79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10f3398-95fb-51ac-819d-349fa3583218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462315} {:answer/id #uuid "93d77f90-d255-502e-a3d2-a8904ab3576f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a3385f-3cab-5129-83bb-f7a582ea68df" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a268ac-7559-5f46-bf6c-3d1043ac53f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462317} {:answer/id #uuid "47e07c54-ee13-5022-a287-d6f6ab82ff3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462317} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b82a872-8326-5513-8f4a-ccff55e33bb4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa10a572-2a12-5313-a511-b89c8f024781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462319} {:answer/id #uuid "f79ee182-a849-5b22-a888-28a5c0d2851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462319} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc60cb7-562c-5b04-bbc2-febf547f3bde" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28a570c8-4263-5a25-a7a9-87fb602a6de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343490" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly solution I matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "49ae1a21-0dd4-5aa8-8ab3-c3f8d983ec48" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8588f05c-f259-5f6f-95a4-ba743efb8633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230323} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2a5ac-5e6b-5176-ada3-76165f3827b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230324} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f684b-31be-53dd-959a-4c693200fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230325} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbe1595-b0a0-5005-89d3-b1a66671386d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad71e63-ab40-5319-b40d-20fec2c9d4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c1fc5b-ecb6-509b-ab8e-ba64d464ed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2dc0571-7d63-58c4-a7e9-b264e1dac3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343760" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "60cce0c1-556a-5961-8f98-6d5844f01954"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ca90e4-8cb7-55b8-a354-4e9dc097fb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355277} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd7fbc0-1387-5d34-8f46-c4279237b187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355354} {:answer/id #uuid "80d2b946-bf3f-50ed-94d6-6429fb7fcc6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355796} {:answer/id #uuid "93972c70-73ca-5e80-9504-616aadd0051d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355966} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c23ba-58b1-5366-a8c3-d53ce428b496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356647} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae144dc-0f56-5164-b0eb-64f56e70b5d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07acc17d-166d-54d0-8f9f-eb0556f3a46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94235" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=7\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{7}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{7}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{7}.$"} :step/id #uuid "abd504bd-fe3b-56bf-9421-c152e2c9477e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaa23f1-3d6f-5700-8adf-0ab757d01cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b00e565-5dc1-5b1f-8a94-9d5cc2730e81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343699" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af750ac2-1d2a-5385-920d-12fa70467b02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481702}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481572}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483509} :activity/id #uuid "0521a0d5-f5e3-5c81-bc28-29faeb852deb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435704} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343673" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{3}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03961e7e-a825-52c6-937e-d6714eaba4ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "498bed50-28c7-50fa-a5df-0080495261c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abd7c36-14a4-56ef-ada9-6c206b80a9d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "968f331f-040e-5a16-a459-23a91c08dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343473" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5ba3c2fb-de20-5932-96c6-e28e4bb83716" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d69105-ab2d-55c3-9612-ee987e634694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8b9ffd-965a-5c5b-8676-57da40fbb9a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31720ab0-69d4-52f5-b3cb-fcd1e2ec0d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467258} {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b86baf-0e5a-524c-b103-8dac5824fae3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} {:answer/id #uuid "6239f5bd-e810-5e80-93e2-7e27a749a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5a3574-1760-5b93-87fd-b5f37a0870f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "65d89567-4925-56ab-9bcd-c0152afe9d34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8588f05c-f259-5f6f-95a4-ba743efb8633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230323} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2a5ac-5e6b-5176-ada3-76165f3827b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230324} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f684b-31be-53dd-959a-4c693200fbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230325} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbe1595-b0a0-5005-89d3-b1a66671386d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad71e63-ab40-5319-b40d-20fec2c9d4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230326} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c1fc5b-ecb6-509b-ab8e-ba64d464ed59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2dc0571-7d63-58c4-a7e9-b264e1dac3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343760" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "aa755d32-f5f0-5147-8e10-608442403c58"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5551b954-fda2-5f2d-abc7-527dd2646792" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe5e8487-7f26-563a-8df2-2908b3b409a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483460}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482797}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495189} :activity/id #uuid "45da9bbc-1512-5651-8f30-bc8c978f6a42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1673e8-dffa-58ad-a21d-370ac4f21d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18cc19c-c2dd-5d83-85f6-24d5473b0766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64d62ce0-8437-5a77-8100-0fa04bf9d1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343690" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=20\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04593abc-de35-5ea9-a146-5ea660c1a706" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3210051c-3999-5418-903e-b15e38e02322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333450} {:answer/id #uuid "38b5b020-4f8f-567f-a7cc-df5b75d4aa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333513} {:answer/id #uuid "64c4f9a2-40a9-51cb-89ef-5dd5f4612358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333903} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9c39-59bb-537d-b57b-efe61e7a1d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334306} {:answer/id #uuid "bb46ab48-8ea8-59c5-8936-9b90e3e9cf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334306} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f96a3-e6a8-5ed6-8d03-4b9a5051d156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cf0fed1-cc47-5050-81b5-0e3536d5ca7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64647" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "2265a2b8-a125-50ed-bfc1-8d6afde6c2a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b13814-4c69-5bb6-8b57-73e807680e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354754} {:answer/id #uuid "5099e87a-dff9-5c35-b894-e19a23671e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355384} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed0d800-100d-5331-be07-cf9b7d1f922b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355645} {:answer/id #uuid "7cddcd84-f830-5b88-a16f-f75d5f927c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355763} {:answer/id #uuid "bff3e8db-ba05-59e7-ae17-59543c35e946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355763} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d8a8347-f78f-5143-a87c-1d1b551dcbcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2cead4d-0d70-5529-b159-16c77413f78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94238" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{6}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{6}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{6}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3fe268b2-a12f-5127-8880-a5efc009faa7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4de519-7dc3-5a63-91dc-88fb097de927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e5b3fd-f8d6-55a8-9a84-9bc4da788fc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c255544-1d5d-5734-8a75-e3f42b3984e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343679" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "683c98ce-5d82-50d3-a3f8-d24ea690b317" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ac45f2-8682-5e38-90a5-af6b3b0df052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442734} {:answer/id #uuid "720de89b-25be-516b-9df5-32bca90894e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442735} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3b3ddc-f2b0-5960-a120-6e3182615388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} {:answer/id #uuid "90774d44-8df7-5a01-b829-eff4553b5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442737} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5401b3-3249-566f-8e5f-01020ae0590b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442736} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3260424-876c-50ae-ac8b-7b96731c9907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee642054-1d27-50a4-8f05-345ca578dfc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343743" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 9D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c95a38fe-1860-539e-b737-8be81b3f72b1"}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494304}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518259} :activity/id #uuid "4d9716c2-538f-569a-b8a0-0013d0209d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd368cc-9a2b-551e-b564-ee313f513e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517541} {:answer/id #uuid "20deeaf4-0740-55aa-9dcb-2e6f140632ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517545} {:answer/id #uuid "79c50765-9d84-524e-b588-8c069de3e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517717} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d57042-0b74-581c-8b12-7c36887caccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} {:answer/id #uuid "fb517908-0db1-516e-8b98-2030ddb3dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff11dfc-8ed3-50df-b798-7c071f983a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" :question/math-academy-id "q-343666" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "373883cf-0b34-53c8-a1b3-d0897fad83b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518266}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a645be0-c56e-5fba-b4a4-a98456908295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517602} {:answer/id #uuid "47adda2a-334d-5928-b439-90caf6b3f1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517627} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf5111e-dd21-52f0-ba55-7a49bf7ac9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517657} {:answer/id #uuid "8227aecf-efb6-5968-88a7-e86a8151635a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517736} {:answer/id #uuid "b985565e-b931-58c3-9ed9-2657a34e2b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517657} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb900ce9-eab3-5acd-b944-c83320b552a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6127495a-5135-5ebc-930f-1823afa46170" :question/math-academy-id "q-94240" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{3}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{3}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "6ea7ee19-1db2-5615-9f9e-e31df6e39383" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1673e8-dffa-58ad-a21d-370ac4f21d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18cc19c-c2dd-5d83-85f6-24d5473b0766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64d62ce0-8437-5a77-8100-0fa04bf9d1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343690" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=20\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88f88f3d-b294-5d78-b139-7b4adde6210f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8deee66-8faa-5103-9ffb-a0528de31c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467280} {:answer/id #uuid "8efe3b1c-13b5-58c1-8319-811c069ec943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467281} :answer-field/id #uuid "791e5bec-2bbe-5ef0-be17-7936c37a397d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b11162f-ed10-562f-a582-635aca5f072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467282} {:answer/id #uuid "c1df0d29-07b6-5d2b-a737-504fb58038dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467283} :answer-field/id #uuid "930db752-9805-5bd5-8731-4cec255544bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db48d1bd-0f55-5b7e-8868-e1d284da68cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467284} {:answer/id #uuid "1a443ce3-df82-501c-a748-ef96d7ff6e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467285} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f14578-0fa8-5566-9fd0-14c07a82fade" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7120fcf0-d3de-513a-93a7-21786701d7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343764" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "d88d67e5-0c3f-5914-b3bd-6949d7868fd1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaa23f1-3d6f-5700-8adf-0ab757d01cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b00e565-5dc1-5b1f-8a94-9d5cc2730e81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343699" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f52a8e3f-5139-540f-bde0-174c3dc62d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518302}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517486}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153280} :activity/id #uuid "180bc304-f210-5e84-909c-4bbc79e4ed9f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099 :topic/id #uuid "da82e0bb-7193-5348-9d38-5f5b8f5913d8" :topic/math-academy-id 2741} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153379 :tutorial/content "Remember that the general solution of the ODE\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' + 6y = 0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\nNow, let's consider the same ODE with some additional conditions imposed:\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' + 6y = 0, \\quad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nThis is called an **initial value problem (IVP)**. In addition to the differential equation, we are given two **initial conditions**:\n\n$$\ny(0) = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y'(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nThe goal of an IVP is to use these initial conditions to find specific values for the arbitrary constants $A$ and $B,$ which gives us a unique *particular solution*.\n\nIn the next slide, we will use the initial conditions to solve for $A$ and $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9ef9a04-db4f-5a03-9f88-1decc97d37a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4189 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a3066dbe-519f-5489-801f-7c0f0adac64d" :step/math-academy-id 593800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151994 :question/id #uuid "4739ced1-bbcc-516b-971f-a580ec97961a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431541}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"} :step/id #uuid "eec105fa-8b77-52c4-942c-421615c540a8" :step/math-academy-id 593801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151995 :question/id #uuid "95cdd4cc-df70-5dac-8bd3-66edb012cdcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6862"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476916}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"} :step/id #uuid "654f7930-b590-5a59-aceb-5c1871a888b2" :step/math-academy-id 593804 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151983 :question/id #uuid "88fe37cf-5df2-5988-90b8-082f7aaca609" :question/math-academy-id "e-6791"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476932}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"} :step/id #uuid "c996e0f5-de06-5eff-b757-1dda970e9711" :step/math-academy-id 593807 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153284}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152008 :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431536}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"} :step/id #uuid "d654529f-589d-5d0d-8f0c-d36887cd5543" :step/math-academy-id 593810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153337 :tutorial/content "Let's find the particular solution for the **initial value problem**:\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' + 6y =0, \\ \\ y(0) = 1, \\ \\ y' (0) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe start with the general solution:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Apply the first initial condition, $y(0)=1.$**\nSubstituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution gives our first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Apply the second initial condition, $y'(0)=1.$**\nFirst, we need to find the derivative, $y'(x){:}$\n\n$$\ny'(x) =  -2 A e ^ { -2 x}  -3 B e ^ {-3 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y'=1$ gives our second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =-2A{e}^{0}-3B{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =-2A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Solve the system of equations for $A$ and $B.$**\nWe have a system of two linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ 1 &=-2A-3B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we can write $A=1-B.$ Substituting this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2(1-B)-3B \\\\ 1 & =-2+2B-3B \\\\ 3 & =-B \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, find $A{:}$ \n\n$$\nA = 1-B = 1 - (-3) = 4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Write the particular solution.**\nSubstituting $A=4$ and $B=-3$ back into the general solution gives the unique solution to the IVP:\n\n$$\ny(x) = 4e^{-2x} - 3 e^{-3x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b54e0bd-604d-58a8-b709-f4681ddc82e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14883 :tutorial/title "Solving the Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "8d53dc21-f0d8-55e4-b7d0-bde13d7a2021" :step/math-academy-id 607933 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153281}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous Initial Value Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152360}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476995} :activity/id #uuid "3dfbf288-a291-5393-a094-9be13293a8e2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099 :topic/id #uuid "da82e0bb-7193-5348-9d38-5f5b8f5913d8" :topic/math-academy-id 2741} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f302d50-9ca1-586e-b1f2-bb16774e9a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476342} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1af17e-b2e8-5dca-b48a-32244e7c918d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476481} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a0dcd-a0d2-5099-876c-b65c9f336a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476540} {:answer/id #uuid "ca922ede-2598-5bbd-91ed-2c115cc7ea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476658} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd949c2-5044-5076-a445-a9bbc18e3e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdeabe96-7dc2-5825-b44a-1ef489407f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a134fdbb-21b9-55b4-a24e-2df884a5e2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81396" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x,\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-5B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5=5A\\,⟹\\,A=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1306b1e8-f6cc-5b8f-9885-e46a06e6b7d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de3cea3-9ce1-5117-8440-1c848ea22d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355356} {:answer/id #uuid "82b9aacb-816c-533f-8f18-7bad4adc1c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355814} {:answer/id #uuid "90f20f88-ad66-5e68-9ff3-f7c959e067cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355943} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a0b4b1-1e9c-550c-b5cf-d7579feaa3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356089} {:answer/id #uuid "f366d31e-4d24-5d08-beff-fd3ad156a6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356793} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdfe16cc-d998-5278-adcf-1b050e572699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ecd297d2-0d03-5232-81da-fdcaeb168238" :question/math-academy-id "q-94030" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{-3x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3P+2Q=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "175938d0-fece-5a13-994e-7f2a8a04c450" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476986}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "501007c1-1237-54a3-8146-49746a54174b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}-4x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476368} {:answer/id #uuid "6133a790-bf37-5624-a5e5-d93d8aac76a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476424} {:answer/id #uuid "90703a07-a49f-5b3e-9f80-5fb5897a85ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476512} {:answer/id #uuid "e78a3592-edab-5dca-829b-212313e22fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}+4x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476720} {:answer/id #uuid "f6418223-cfc1-5b18-8627-68adb9ed9afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{x}+8x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca796d4b-8681-5245-bc1d-c35792d5e364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e57d61a-2fa5-55d2-aed6-295e67c97f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-130350" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=4.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=8,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nA+B=8.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=4$ gives $B=4.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{x}+4x{e}^{x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5749455f-9503-59fa-a876-a1ec4ac2fd2e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "505e2df8-db79-59e7-a583-99babc506741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263697} {:answer/id #uuid "5654280f-88bb-59f1-b9d2-19091ada66cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263743} {:answer/id #uuid "79b176c8-c019-5f2d-89b9-2523faf24214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264055} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e423e9-b332-5cd9-a6ed-b6e6e50a5c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264852} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0ac67-5324-577a-afe9-536d41e51116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263743} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf4956a-8f92-55a5-93db-74651c950270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c95764b1-5855-5fb2-b3ad-f0b7d5850b93" :question/math-academy-id "q-130374" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-P+Q=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=0.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b359008-56cc-5644-a2dc-31e108447e77"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16afed35-5463-5e89-b46a-71267d7d4dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333191} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d2b579-81ed-55f0-86c7-1541a3ed079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334660} {:answer/id #uuid "f16df0e0-2094-5727-8779-eedca1e17dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335105} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2ea755-747f-5b1b-81ec-4430d746d47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335202} {:answer/id #uuid "fc44ffb2-d97c-5268-8493-a8451eaabd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335105} :answer-field/id #uuid "87dc976c-f470-53a5-ae6e-a363a677d1ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0006dc55-4e80-5e2f-bc47-1762d506390e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65117" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x,\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-4B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4=4A\\,⟹\\,A=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1685e0e-fc49-5045-b7b4-a59aeaeaad30" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476979}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous Initial Value Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476199}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390} :activity/id #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3522 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4620379b-2eed-5dd7-bf3a-9edf0b4aea79" :step/math-academy-id 19988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519985} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "77230797-e80e-5644-8b1e-9e3722a0b66d" :step/math-academy-id 19991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7ebf299f-89ad-55ae-8fe6-d0af3bc56dfd" :step/math-academy-id 37508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446379}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "debf1d2f-c0e6-5c61-b195-a95710e2c35e" :step/math-academy-id 37509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679} :activity/id #uuid "26e5acff-8db9-5e5d-9122-36baf139baac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b28520-430a-5247-a200-fd5f414e5ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} {:answer/id #uuid "f8033251-ab93-5bb9-a145-e5e99be3d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3c91e0-c158-561e-ab32-6e72b9ba78b5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647ee441-bfd6-5c35-84c4-0fc1e7479328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e66778-9876-5718-a6e2-1e95fc527bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} :answer-field/id #uuid "030534f1-c9cc-5372-9ff2-bdc749c441e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d47ce5-4beb-5f68-bae7-0d361ea95c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454016} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dcdf0f-841a-5b0d-aff9-db735d828b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017} :answer-field/id #uuid "eced16f4-9e93-50d2-bf88-cdda3960f7ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "42d8e8b3-7048-5f94-884b-ae373fb59042" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f87f060-5b8c-5b4a-806f-8cf93ee4d70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449634} {:answer/id #uuid "4360a160-1403-523e-8e62-4fbb2ec722d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449635} :answer-field/id #uuid "359ebe96-94aa-58ea-b60b-be863ac84a52" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb552ac2-1033-5cf0-95e3-c64844f7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449636} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cf0040-9b44-519e-ab54-532b2b101a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449637} :answer-field/id #uuid "65159a93-fba0-5a45-abcc-86fe85e1adb6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39e5820e-dedb-509b-bef8-5b24f4aea7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449638} {:answer/id #uuid "319a4d81-4806-58b2-9a6b-94494e8c5b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449638} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8aa8650-12f8-56c0-ac84-85ed73892db0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9a467517-2fb7-54c3-b455-e47e7392ef56"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235a52da-656a-5cf2-a401-0f42a3642391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} {:answer/id #uuid "04790f9e-17da-5ed1-82d4-a7391bccbd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ff1b3-8e47-5988-b4a7-49adadda7718" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39055a2-7be3-5968-8135-398e60211c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459825} {:answer/id #uuid "f28e5027-719a-5804-a84a-81f68d441b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ef0960-7c5f-543c-a81b-bf7733be9b0f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3932a553-bb8b-5851-b93b-ecabd828b7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} {:answer/id #uuid "909e13f6-b658-585b-af87-0fb072eb5e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c740ae4-9a04-5de5-a467-c4e121394796" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5b63a26-4713-5ecd-9d64-befaccc0bd77" :question/math-academy-id "q-34834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d2193342-5bc1-5a6f-99d1-42bf1fad6047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435669}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485604} :activity/id #uuid "7e6f49d0-b97d-5a05-9f9c-64559727441e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d5ed8d-7e60-5db5-b7d1-d96bb65185a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474362} {:answer/id #uuid "10b14067-3f7f-5868-8791-43396e0607b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb207fd4-1ade-5c72-8528-ba143a738870" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0948d764-f192-5b49-80f0-17ce4b9bcefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aade65-5d3a-520b-86ea-745a85070e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b6ddd3-8ce7-5c7f-874e-23d5563021dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dc218b-818c-594c-978b-8b69736909ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474366} {:answer/id #uuid "4999264b-817a-578e-85f5-209e47509eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ecf0bb-4395-5d51-99bd-1c0a67d30176" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068" :question/math-academy-id "q-139978" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero\n\nIII. $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1140fa5b-f47a-5b21-a507-87491673347d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06680c4f-a621-5dc3-875e-3896db2af04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1cf54f-05d3-5550-8f3d-cdfa3137d5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460023} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3156979-45c5-5d24-80c1-f665fce48ba8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c8b9c9-048e-580b-90b5-0615fcacbfe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460025} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd76941-0308-57d6-9b7b-3ba6c48171e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460026} :answer-field/id #uuid "44325e4a-3b71-5949-9822-a4ed24085c3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3989b63d-47f8-5757-bb11-6a685f029eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} {:answer/id #uuid "16bc9a89-2517-5c10-b196-866970cad27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460027} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9665add-7abd-5377-b1f0-9d3eafc436be" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f4e1182-d195-5cca-bb1a-601ea1f9c921" :question/math-academy-id "q-47599" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}$\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is an $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1b87589b-dcb5-58d7-a287-28c231b8ee26" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f8d9af-560e-5e04-801f-49c3569ea09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464240} {:answer/id #uuid "3683cdbb-cea0-5d3a-88ae-46bbf35523a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464240} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8b8cb5-e75e-518a-91a0-160dae2c2bc4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bba33a3-e841-5573-8abf-90651ed20a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464242} {:answer/id #uuid "0c34db9f-4df3-5319-9c26-896a5b27e435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464242} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d917c1-327f-5ee0-8aed-ef99e81a107b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2f17c6-86e7-560e-b3af-0ab0f7aab9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464244} {:answer/id #uuid "06de2a3a-d315-52ff-898b-03d434ea28ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464245} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bcd47e5-e550-5069-9017-2362f6f7a623" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-142358" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n- **I.** $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2fdad5e3-18b8-51dd-aadf-07ba0badc4d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b28520-430a-5247-a200-fd5f414e5ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} {:answer/id #uuid "f8033251-ab93-5bb9-a145-e5e99be3d388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454012} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3c91e0-c158-561e-ab32-6e72b9ba78b5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "647ee441-bfd6-5c35-84c4-0fc1e7479328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e66778-9876-5718-a6e2-1e95fc527bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454014} :answer-field/id #uuid "030534f1-c9cc-5372-9ff2-bdc749c441e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d47ce5-4beb-5f68-bae7-0d361ea95c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454016} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dcdf0f-841a-5b0d-aff9-db735d828b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454017} :answer-field/id #uuid "eced16f4-9e93-50d2-bf88-cdda3960f7ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not invertible\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\n- **III.** $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a6dcb993-e91b-5b36-8076-9c0539317c39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3d0a6-c269-5dde-bafd-a91861927c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426036} {:answer/id #uuid "1848f756-51a0-5639-8084-5aa59abdaebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426037} {:answer/id #uuid "562361ed-d031-5fb2-9175-4571a3994648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426047} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7285e7-4a5d-5073-aa0f-ffd3853ddc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "lI only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426054} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d58843-37c3-5988-8dac-72db97dcd1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec9750c-eaeb-571d-b465-6fa1ab05e88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fbccd10-1c43-51a4-ad8b-36063e11427d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b314981c-1803-59e7-b442-57ac10b70607"}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484809}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529204} :activity/id #uuid "266f8ba6-aa38-58ed-8d33-3aec933a1e12" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d248bbb6-50b4-5d61-a95e-58f318131503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464246} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5d0dfe-318f-5100-ac82-83455377b98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464247} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d37791a-a285-5a2f-af41-bcd2d8afc075" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3957e353-c39b-5011-af11-d2c9b6787af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464248} {:answer/id #uuid "8a540690-cbd6-5a7b-8e6a-735ce1ef7a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464248} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfe4de2-b1c4-55bb-a60d-27c7be7257dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6788d6-5506-57b7-9972-0d793d0b67f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464250} {:answer/id #uuid "67197e1a-6b46-5a73-9b9e-21d0f7711026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464251} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d8c4a3-f095-50cd-bcf7-92ff6b429b75" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142475" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $A$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0a7d2e71-2df5-5616-a66c-1039ae6b7480"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0bd5dd-c5f7-5e5d-b21c-bcdb4e2b830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528560} {:answer/id #uuid "7385e402-bf26-5641-9a0b-660e87ee9d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528567} {:answer/id #uuid "e0746457-062b-51a1-b39b-1801dcc9dd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528849} {:answer/id #uuid "e15f2509-6136-5ef5-b754-59b129df8ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528852} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fb30e2-e856-5197-b6a2-1b2e5bd62f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528849} :answer-field/id #uuid "255a02e5-2e2c-5989-905e-69f5223274a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74f4be02-4fe9-5741-a4d1-7268f77a2f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142472" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2a21ddf3-d51f-5117-8439-7fd92a4b2b2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d885553-3afc-5696-a382-4c860965f60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528520} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf00a72-f79f-582d-9aaa-38874ff20b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528592} {:answer/id #uuid "7df12baa-7b1b-58dc-8f63-3871724e61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528598} {:answer/id #uuid "c974d506-c2bc-5e7b-97a4-c51ae5371031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528794} {:answer/id #uuid "e94c076f-c6c7-517d-8c55-d74158a4a6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528520} :answer-field/id #uuid "20580d7d-28aa-5c7c-a048-784d0bfa2d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1d7f6a4-6114-5671-a52c-7df48bee5e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-141342" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately. \n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times $ \n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn, means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0,$ we must have that $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n},$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "94778ac8-3dd7-5564-88c5-443046b2e6fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0a6001-3780-5910-af1b-80f1dc86bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449628} {:answer/id #uuid "711f6d61-9af9-514f-ab7b-3f6b87059a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449628} :answer-field/id #uuid "577301d3-bda4-5711-b8ea-c8d7c59e3764" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd05ec0-7412-5317-bd1f-6fdd48ffae9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449630} {:answer/id #uuid "7e775f74-22c8-5e41-92ca-9b7679c5d763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449631} :answer-field/id #uuid "11747fa2-61fa-56a5-ab66-ef191899dff3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f12245-1e0f-5cf0-8c20-1a321c65854b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449632} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93e8a1-abd3-5bae-8c25-a6156dc77f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449633} :answer-field/id #uuid "71691d56-9200-5496-becc-0f211d28a39b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- **II.** $A$ is not invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cb8b82a3-c3e9-567b-8dbd-19ce7dce93de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d1fafe-67a4-5304-b42b-0fa34a95eaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467578} {:answer/id #uuid "c76c9ea4-d5d0-5c09-adc5-db52b1568fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467579} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df948ff-c1ab-538f-b99e-ed07682677fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6428bf6c-1cd8-523e-89a2-bbd6aefca97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} {:answer/id #uuid "49e7f113-867c-5ee0-a738-ba5b88651827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467580} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8c77e6-a0b4-526b-bf8e-a435b7b66ac1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b22f319-2182-5f2f-984d-2c69a38c95bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} {:answer/id #uuid "f8184f79-583b-5721-92be-e461d3dc1a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58f3fca-628f-5dd4-9837-348f91ad595e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c600e13-fdff-5526-82d0-6659ac10dfc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-47587" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times $\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e46ea0a8-4991-5f8c-a308-9bc68c5b0d80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529203}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528296}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292} :activity/id #uuid "b218a927-38fe-5672-a4d0-f4c57476e22f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125733 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the equation of the **tangent line** to the ellipse \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} =1\n$$\n\n at the point $P(3,1).$\n\nTo solve this problem, we could find the slope of the line using techniques from single-variable calculus. However, we can also solve the problem by computing the gradient of a two-variable function.\n\nLet's start by defining \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac {y^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, the given ellipse is the level curve  $f(x,y)=1.$\n\nNow, we know that $\\nabla \\! f(3,1)$ is a vector that's normal to the level curve $f(x,y)= 1$ at $P(3,1).$ So, it's also normal to the tangent line to our ellipse at $P.$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc75e8fd4083af1bf90f46b2e875d5d552bffdc5c1cfaeabebef70f79f00828.png)\n\nSuppose that $(x,y)$ is any point on that tangent line. Then $\\mathbf v=\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle$ is a vector that's parallel to the tangent line. So $\\mathbf v$ must be also orthogonal to $\\nabla f(3,1),$ and since the dot product of two orthogonal vectors is zero, we have that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(3,1)\\,\\cdot \\,\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $\\nabla\\! f$ and evaluating it at the point $P(3,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{x}{6},\\frac{y}{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the equation of our tangent, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-3)+(y-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9dede8-0672-56eb-9084-cf581e3fb05f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7cc9655e-83bb-5f85-8c20-add1d27fb5e0" :step/math-academy-id 11184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521745} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521743}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "204027bd-d606-5131-82eb-717c2823cc14" :step/math-academy-id 11185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125734 :tutorial/content "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ef07808-783a-543e-9217-5d3d0a9ae13a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2497 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6501f944-f162-5a6e-9f8e-4ccc2cd2ce41" :step/math-academy-id 11188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499554}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "1ce011ea-75f6-5023-9ba5-8deb530c69ba" :step/math-academy-id 11189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521876} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521870}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "6e185929-7cee-53dd-9616-e86a123783e5" :step/math-academy-id 11192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125807 :tutorial/content "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3866 :tutorial/title "Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2e3c389c-2b5c-55ca-92a8-e52fcbcf9f84" :step/math-academy-id 22698 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293}}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124732}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482621} :activity/id #uuid "12b9dfa6-9b29-5bbc-991a-88ee2275afa9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482005} {:answer/id #uuid "73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482042} {:answer/id #uuid "8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482072} {:answer/id #uuid "b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482203} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482072} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :question/math-academy-id "q-130203" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbb66c-c87e-532e-b215-79cebdd50220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321343} {:answer/id #uuid "25737ab8-5934-517a-82e3-355f226249ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321438} {:answer/id #uuid "569fd915-b891-5273-a763-b23c030c775d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321831} {:answer/id #uuid "91bfacf0-e81a-591f-b5c7-eb843413fffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c24a19-a0b8-5781-9f03-f3684c7d7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2328f82b-ecf4-5e93-897c-028b4fd16484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12b276e2-b703-55f7-94b8-069326b3f510" :question/math-academy-id "q-37516" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b898a6-e9f5-51f8-8993-b6f0322af5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403573} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac861-9675-5531-8e0f-29301bd84beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403574} {:answer/id #uuid "879099ba-26c3-5eca-b156-1546984f09e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7a278-62d5-5448-adb4-7672d93764ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403576} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b876c-2af7-5756-8122-0570c4918dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c59322-d13f-5287-a0fa-c2f86091a95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49dc4b38-854b-53b5-ba44-1bf47c27ef1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72412" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uui